[[File:Xàlas.jpg|thumb|300x300px|The commune of [[Xàlas]] ("peace") in the region of [[Campostella]], along the [[Transmatarrañya high speed rail line]]]]
'''Angostura''' ({{Literal translation|the narrows}}), or the '''Angostura Pass''' is a Matarrañyan [[W:Mountain pass|mountain pass]] which has been used since colonial times to link the [[Marañón basin]] on the west side of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] with the [[Baliscan Levante]], avoiding the x-day, {{convert|510.80|km|mi|0|abbr=on}} detour through the less challenging [[Guadazequias valley]] (which, [[Convention of Jatiel|until 1813]], was outside of colonial or Baliscan control). In 1828 it was used by the [[Army of the Matarrañya]] to cross the mountain range, in their [[Siege of Castejón|campaign to seize]] the [[Alpujarra]] and split the [[Northern Confederation]].

Since the 1880s, it has been the most used pass in all of the [[Northern Triangle]]. Today, a railroad tunnel built by the [[Baliscan Railways Group|Transmatarrañyan Railway]] (1910–present) runs underneath, while the [[Autopista 208]] runs through the nearby [[La Joya Tunnel]]''.''

